      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let the two numbers be \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with \\(x > y\\).\n\nWe are given two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the difference of squares formula, which states:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given sum \\(x + y = 6\\) into the formula:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 6(x - y)\n\\]\n\nWe know from the second piece of information that \\(x^2 - y^2 = 12\\), so we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x - y\\):\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(x + y = 6\\)\n2. \\(x - y = 2\\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n2x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(x = 4\\) back into the first equation to find \\(y\\):\n\\[\n4 + y = 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 6 - 4\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2\n\\]\n\nThe two numbers are \\(x = 4\\) and \\(y = 2\\). The positive difference between the two numbers is:\n\\[\n|x - y| = |4 - 2| = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
